  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID Higher Secondary Education Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  In Bangladesh, over 90 percent of students in grades 11 and 12 are enrolled in private high schools. The number of students enrolled in higher secondary education increased by 4.9 percent from 2016 to 2020, reflecting a positive trend. However, despite the increase in enrollment, the quality of higher secondary education has not kept pace. The Government of Bangladesh has identified several gaps that impact student learning: (1) the lack of teacher capacity to effectively teach and prepare students with 21st century skills; (2) the lack of appropriate teacher training materials; (3) inadequate teacher training institutions; (4) soft and transferable skills that are not taught in schools; and (6) education officers and supervisors who lack the required capacity to manage the system and support teachers. USAID&apos;s Higher Secondary Education activity will follow a system strengthening approach to improve the quality of teaching for Bangladeshi secondary school students to reduce these gaps. With FY 2024 funds, the activity will train teachers, principals, vice principals, and education officers on instructional, management, and supervision skills; develop a comprehensive online learning management system; and pilot innovative approaches to enhance learning of marginalized and disadvantaged students, especially for girls in areas of conflict, such as communities hosting refugees. It will also work with relevant government agencies to enhance teachers&apos; instruction and management skills. The activity aligns with CDCS development objective three and ICS MO 3.1 Improved human capital, particularly in health and education. Funding for this activity contributes to the Basic Education ($2m) earmark with Girls in Areas of Conflict ($1.46m), and Private Sector Engagement ($65,000) attributions.</narrative>
  </description>
